finally received the last piece of the vaio svz puzzle (the power brick), and now i can plug the entire setup, but to make it run i needed to touch the gpu drivers and i got bluescreened (lol, so many years since the last one), after some failsafe boots and win7 os rescue, i was able to install everything and use the full potential of the glorious 1gb vram gpu, anyway, i realized i got couple of functions missing, bluetooth and trackpad, fortunately, there's still ONE site on the entire internet (dose.0wnz.at/ftp.vaiolink.com/PUB/VAIO/ORIGINAL/ - dose.0wnz.at/ftp.vaiolink.com/PUB/VAIO/UPDATES/) that hoards all the drivers ever released for any and all vaio's, so after a little information gathering, managed to install everything but bluetooth for some unknown reason, and, suggested by gemini that the driver install order means quite a lot on the vaio's, decided to nuke everything and try again, only to discover after hitting my head with the wall for 4 hours, that for the bluetooth to work you MUST have sony bluetooth bloatware installed, so in a way, i wiped everything for nothing (knowledge it's not for free)

if you ever found yourself with a vaio svz1311c5e that you want to make it shine, here's for your eyes delight the files needed and the install order (there's an oficial 4-discs bundle rescue discs for this model on archive.org btw):

(most of this list was found on vaiolibrary.com)

01_INDCHI-00263910-0042
intel chipset driver (chipset)

02_INDMEI-00267493-0042
intel management engine interface (power controller)

03_INDSAT-00266712-0042
intel mobile express chipset sata raid controller (sata controller)

04_INDUSB-00266909-0042
intel usb 3.0 extensible host controller (usb 3.0)

05_REDETH-00266776-0042
realtek pcie gigabyte ethernet familiy controller (ethernet)

06_INDWLL-00265235-0042
intel centrino advanced n6235 driver (wifi)

07_SOASNW-00267602-0042
sony vaio smart network (bluetooth enabler)

08_INDBLT-00264017-004
intel centrino bluetooth 4.0 + high speed adapter update (bluetooth driver)

09_REDMCC-00266226-0042
realtek pcie card reader driver (integrated sd card reader port)

10_RNDUSB-00263953-0042
renesas electronics dock usb 3.0 host controller (dock controller)

11_MRDOTH-00248942-0042
generic marvell 61xx raid controller for docking station (usb port dock)

12_SODOTH-00264792-0042
sony firmware extension parser device driver (sony bios comms)

13_SOASSL-00264711-0042
sony shared library (sony utilities library)

14_SOAOTH-61000000-0042
sony hotkey utilities (keyboard hotkey functions)

15_EP0000605001-vaioControlCenter_649-08230
vaio control center (vaio control center)

16_SPDTPD-00267971-0042
synaptics PS/2 port touchpad (trackpad)

17_AUDFPD-00264020-0042
authentec aes1660 (fingerprint sensor)

18_AUAOTH-00266716-0042
authentec truesuite software (fingerprint manager)

19_A2DVID-00268409-0042
amd radeon hd 6600 & intel hd graphics 4000 driver bundle (gpus)

20_INAWID-00267860-0042
intel widi software

21_EP0000600623_remoteKeyboard_120-09270
remote keyboard with bravia

22_EP0000297995_remoteKeyboardPS3_121-05220
remote keyboard with playstation3

23_EP0000600291_remotePlayPS3Update_110-15071
remote file access with playstation3

24_EP0000600842_vaioLocationUtility_1100-06060
sony vaio location utility (profile switching by wifi change -printer, shared folders, etc-)

----

99_intel_hd4000_win64_153343.4425
intel hd graphics 4000 driver (last version for intel hd4000 on win7, use if not gonna use the external dock, it's the most stable version)

----

after win7 is full stock and sports full native functionality again (received it with a useless clean w10), turned my attention to the debian dual boot over raid0, good thing that was a problem i solved already on arrival on another endless troubleshoot session, just in case you also want to dualboot yours, heres the tutorial:

====================================

1. Fundamental Architecture:
The hardware uses a RAID0 volume managed by Intel (mdadm). The partitions will be identified as /dev/md/Volume0_0pX or /dev/md126pX.

BIOS Restriction (InsydeH2O): The firmware ignores standard EFI paths added by Linux. It will always try to execute: \EFI\Microsoft\Boot\bootmgfw.efi on the first EFI partition (FAT32 format, p1).

Shim Dependency: If the signed Debian bootloader is used, it strictly looks for grubx64.efi in its own directory.

2. Initial Installation
Install linux on the unpartitioned space that was left after windows install (I choosed Debian):

During partitioning, if the installer creates a second EFI partition on its own, it doesn't matter.

Complete the linux install. The vaio svz will boot straight into win7 still, so boot from the installation USB again.

3. You need to fix the grub since sony bios is a bitch (grub is fine, it's sony bios what fucks around), so on the installation main window, enter the terminal and sudo yourself:

# 1. Load modules and assemble the Intel RAID
[CODE]
modprobe ext4
modprobe vfat
mdadm --assemble --scan
[CODE]

# 2. Mount the Debian(linux) system (adjust the root partition number)
# IT IS NECESSARY TO MOUNT THE DEBIAN(linux) BOOT PARTITION TO COPY grubx64.efi TO WINDOWS:
# mount the 1GB partition located just before the main Debian(linux) partition that contains grubx64.efi (replace X with actual partition number that contains grubx64.efi)
[CODE]
mount /dev/md126pX /mnt
[CODE]

# 3. Bind kernel devices
[CODE]
mount --bind /dev /mnt/dev
mount --bind /dev/pts /mnt/dev/pts
mount --bind /proc /mnt/proc
mount --bind /sys /mnt/sys
[CODE]

# 4. Enter the installed system
[CODE]
chroot /mnt
[CODE]

4. Connect the first EFI partition (p1) and rearrange the binaries:
# 1. Now mount the Windows EFI partition (p1) to a temporary folder
[CODE]
mkdir -p /mnt/win_efi
mount -t vfat /dev/md126p1 /mnt/win_efi
[CODE]

# 2. Backup the original win7 bootloader
[CODE]
cp /mnt/win_efi/EFI/Microsoft/Boot/bootmgfw.efi /mnt/win_efi/EFI/Microsoft/Boot/bootmgfw.efi.bak
[CODE]

# 3. Copy the GRUB binary both under its own name and replacing the Windows one
# (Use the actual Debian grubx64.efi to avoid failed shim dependencies)
[CODE]
cp /boot/efi/EFI/debian/grubx64.efi /mnt/win_efi/EFI/Microsoft/Boot/grubx64.efi
cp /boot/efi/EFI/debian/grubx64.efi /mnt/win_efi/EFI/Microsoft/Boot/bootmgfw.efi
[CODE]

# 4. Create the bridge file to redirect GRUB to the root partition
[CODE]
nano /mnt/win_efi/EFI/Microsoft/Boot/grub.cfg
[CODE]

Add this content to the grub.cfg file:

[CODE]
	search.file /boot/grub/grub.cfg root
	set prefix=($root)/boot/grub
	configfile $prefix/grub.cfg
[CODE]

# 5. Unmount
[CODE]
umount /mnt/win_efi
[CODE]

5. Dual Boot Menu Configuration (GRUB)

Configure Debian(linux) to display the grub and allow choosing between Linux and Win7:
Ensure menu visibility in /etc/default/grub:
[CODE]
nano /etc/default/grub
[CODE]

Add these lines:
[CODE]
    GRUB_TIMEOUT_STYLE=menu
    GRUB_TIMEOUT=5
    GRUB_DISABLE_OS_PROBER=false
    GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT=""
[CODE]

Add the manual Win7 entry in /etc/grub.d/40_custom:
[CODE]
nano /etc/grub.d/40_custom
[CODE]

Add at the end:
[CODE]
menuentry "Windows 7" --class windows --class os {
    insmod part_gpt
    insmod fat
    insmod chain
    search --no-floppy --file --set=root /EFI/Microsoft/Boot/bootmgfw.efi.bak
    chainloader /EFI/Microsoft/Boot/bootmgfw.efi.bak
}
[CODE]

Apply changes and reboot:
[CODE]
update-grub
reboot
[CODE]

====================

and just incase you want to image the raid0 SO YOU NEVER HAVE TO DEAL WITH THIS SHIT EVER FUCKING AGAIN (like me), here's the clonezilla guide for that juicy proprietary ssd:

USE CLONEZILLA UBUNTU FLAVOUR EXCLUSIVELY:

1.
on clonezilla main menu, launch a command line, then:

[CODE]
sudo su -
export IMSM_NO_PLATFORM=1
mdadm -A --scan --verbose
lsblk
[CODE]

you'll get a list with all the partitions from p1 to p5 or p6 or even more, at least 3 for win7 and minimum 2 for Debian(linux), if you are here, you are on the right track.

2.
still on the CLI launch clonezilla gui

clonezilla

select expert mode

'saveparts'
'disk_or_parts'
check all partitions from the presented list (p1 to p5 or p6)
-q2
in advanced extra parameters check --rescue (just in case you have bad sectors, for clonezilla to zero them and complete the imaging)
-z1p
size 0

done

(i assume any and all svz models follows this same procedure)

quite the troubleshoot huh? i'm still impressed i was able to pull this off
